WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.334 --> 00:00:22.173
The next morning, when Mary arrived in Colin's room, she saw that he looked pale and worn out from his temper tantrum.

2
00:00:22.174 --> 00:00:27.912
"I'm glad you came. I ache all over because I'm so tired."

3
00:00:27.913 --> 00:00:33.636
He noticed she was wearing her hat. "Are you going somewhere?"

4
00:00:33.637 --> 00:00:37.825
"I won't be long. I need to talk to Dickon.

5
00:00:37.826 --> 00:00:40.403
It's something about the garden."

6
00:00:40.404 --> 00:00:47.069
"Oh, is it?" Colin said eagerly. "I dreamed about the garden all night."

7
00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:50.027
Mary left him and went outside.

8
00:00:50.028 --> 00:00:53.450
Dickon was already at work in the secret garden.

9
00:00:53.451 --> 00:00:56.626
His animal friends were busy watching him.

10
00:00:56.627 --> 00:01:03.168
Besides the fox and crow, he'd brought along two squirrels named Nut and Shell.

11
00:01:03.169 --> 00:01:07.883
"I rode over on Jump, my moor pony," he told Mary.

12
00:01:07.884 --> 00:01:11.293
"Would you like to see him?"

13
00:01:11.294 --> 00:01:16.518
Dickon led Mary into the woods, where the shaggy pony was eating grass.

14
00:01:16.519 --> 00:01:20.414
The boy and his pony seemed to have a conversation.

15
00:01:20.415 --> 00:01:26.476
Then Dickon made Jump give Mary a kiss on her cheek. She liked that.

16
00:01:26.477 --> 00:01:30.807
Back in the garden, the two children sat under a tree.

17
00:01:30.808 --> 00:01:34.967
Mary began to tell Dickon about Colin's tantrum.

18
00:01:34.968 --> 00:01:40.689
Dickon shook his head sadly. "Just listen to the birds this morning.

19
00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:45.068
The world seems full of them, all whistling and singing.

20
00:01:45.069 --> 00:01:51.431
And that poor boy has been lying shut up in the house, thinking thoughts that made him scream.

21
00:01:51.432 --> 00:01:56.006
We must get him out here, soaking up air and sunshine.

22
00:01:56.007 --> 00:01:58.979
We have no time to lose."

23
00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:02.671
"No, there is no time to lose."

24
00:02:02.672 --> 00:02:07.949
The two of them agreed upon a plan, and then Mary ran back into the house.

25
00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:13.118
When she sat down next to Colin's bed, he sniffed the air nearby.

26
00:02:13.119 --> 00:02:16.800
"You smell like flowers and fresh things!"

27
00:02:16.801 --> 00:02:21.737
"That's the wind from the moor—and the springtime and sunshine."

28
00:02:21.738 --> 00:02:27.637
Colin took another big breath. There was so much to talk about.

29
00:02:27.638 --> 00:02:32.055
He wanted to hear all about Dickon's wild animal friends.

30
00:02:32.056 --> 00:02:38.706
"Well," said Mary. "Today I met Jump, the moor pony.

31
00:02:38.707 --> 00:02:43.089
He trotted right up to Dickon and laid his head on Dickon's shoulder.

32
00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:48.819
Dickon talked into his ear, and Jump answered back with whinnies and snorts!"

33
00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:53.166
"Do you think Jump really understands everything Dickon says?"

34
00:02:53.167 --> 00:02:55.683
"It seems as if he does.

35
00:02:55.684 --> 00:02:59.704
Dickon says anything will understand you if you're friends with it."

36
00:02:59.705 --> 00:03:02.926
Colin lay quietly for a little while.

37
00:03:02.927 --> 00:03:08.052
He seemed to be staring at the wall, but Mary knew he was thinking.

38
00:03:08.053 --> 00:03:12.471
"I wish I were friends with things," he said at last.

39
00:03:12.472 --> 00:03:19.409
"But I'm not. I never had anything to be friends with, and I can't stand people."

40
00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:21.813
"Can you stand me?"

41
00:03:21.814 --> 00:03:26.481
"Yes, I can. It's funny, but I even like you."

42
00:03:26.482 --> 00:03:29.223
Mary was thoughtful too.

43
00:03:29.224 --> 00:03:34.019
"Ben Weatherstaff, the gardener, once said that he and I were alike.

44
00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:36.785
I think you're like us too.

45
00:03:36.786 --> 00:03:41.858
Ben said neither of us was good-looking, and we were as sour as we looked.

46
00:03:41.859 --> 00:03:47.158
But I don't feel so sour anymore, now that I know Dickon and the robin."

47
00:03:47.159 --> 00:03:50.326
"Did you feel as though you hated people?"

48
00:03:50.327 --> 00:03:56.506
"Yes," said Mary. "I would have hated you if I'd seen you back then."

49
00:03:56.507 --> 00:04:00.085
Colin reached out his hand and patted her.

50
00:04:00.086 --> 00:04:05.021
"Mary, I wish I hadn't said what I did about sending Dickon away.

51
00:04:05.022 --> 00:04:09.962
I wouldn't mind him looking at me. I want to see him."

52
00:04:09.963 --> 00:04:14.316
"I'm glad you said that because . . . because . . ."

53
00:04:14.317 --> 00:04:17.812
"Because what?" Colin said eagerly.

54
00:04:17.813 --> 00:04:23.614
Mary was so anxious that she hopped up from her stool and caught hold of both his hands.

55
00:04:23.615 --> 00:04:28.719
"Can I trust you? Can I really trust you?"

56
00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:31.405
"Yes! Yes!"

57
00:04:31.406 --> 00:04:37.606
"Dickon will bring his creatures to see you tomorrow. But that's not all."

58
00:04:37.607 --> 00:04:44.199
Mary was shaking with excitement. "There's a door into the secret garden.

59
00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:47.881
I found it under the ivy on the wall."

60
00:04:47.882 --> 00:04:53.509
If he had been a strong, healthy boy, Colin probably would have jumped up and shouted, "Hooray!"

61
00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:58.093
Instead his eyes grew bigger and he gasped for breath.

62
00:04:58.094 --> 00:05:03.350
"Oh, Mary! Will I see it?" He pulled her toward him.

63
00:05:03.351 --> 00:05:06.516
"Will I live to get inside it?"

64
00:05:06.517 --> 00:05:10.649
"Of course you'll see it," Mary said impatiently.

65
00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:16.014
"Of course you'll live to get inside it. Don't be silly."

66
00:05:16.015 --> 00:05:21.229
She seemed so certain about this that he soon began to laugh at himself.

67
00:05:21.230 --> 00:05:26.058
He asked her to tell him again about the garden and how it might look.

68
00:05:26.059 --> 00:05:31.756
He listened to every detail and soon forgot his aches and tiredness.

69
00:05:31.757 --> 00:05:35.742
"It sounds as if you've really seen it," he said.

70
00:05:35.743 --> 00:05:40.547
Mary hesitated for a minute and then blurted out the truth.

71
00:05:40.548 --> 00:05:44.469
"I found the key and went into the garden weeks ago.

72
00:05:44.470 --> 00:05:52.297
But I didn't dare tell you until I knew for sure that I could trust you."
